      ":question/id": "5d08c35d-51cc-50ea-bb44-1ebd28104462",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-109911",
      ":question/problem": "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}+1,\\,y={v}^{3}+1,\\,z=2u+v.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a surface with parametrization $r(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${r}_{u}^{\u2032}\\times {r}_{v}^{\u2032}.$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=\u27e8{u}^{2}+1,\\,{v}^{3}+1,\\,2u+v\u27e9.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{\u2032} & =\u27e8\\frac{\u2202x}{\u2202u},\\,\\frac{\u2202y}{\u2202u},\\,\\frac{\u2202z}{\u2202u}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202u}({u}^{2}+1),\\,\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202u}({v}^{3}+1),\\,\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202u}(2u+v)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82u,\\,0,\\,2\u27e9 \\\\ {r}_{v}^{\u2032} & =\u27e8\\frac{\u2202x}{\u2202v},\\,\\frac{\u2202y}{\u2202v},\\,\\frac{\u2202z}{\u2202v}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202v}({v}^{3}+1),\\,\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202v}(2u+v)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e80,\\,3{v}^{2},\\,1\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{\u2032}\\times {r}_{v}^{\u2032} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2u & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3{v}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\u27e8|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 3{v}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}2u & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}|\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-6{v}^{2},\\,-2u,\\,6u{v}^{2}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
